3,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,34193,429,220,1124,58,18,20,59,50853,19,15,28,16,19,15,15,7110,60,9885,1045,7546,400,77,3,448,400,15,59,273,80,308,27,16,18,24,24,3,1741,429,400,77,3,374,279,12557,65266,27949,311,220,16,21,15,59864,220,16,18,24,24,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198,1249,1477,279,7546,17767,308,1124,8,1741,429,17767,220,15,1124,273,80,308,366,220,16,18,24,24,1124,8,323,17767,308,1124,8,374,279,12557,65266,27949,315,220,16,21,15,59864,220,16,18,24,24,11,582,1184,311,1477,17767,308,1124,8,1741,429,1447,78045,220,16,21,15,77,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,16,18,24,24,92,1124,2533,1986,3363,582,1184,311,1477,17767,308,1124,8,1741,429,17767,220,16,21,15,77,481,220,16,18,24,24,74,284,220,16,1124,8,369,1045,7546,17767,595,1124,568,1096,374,264,13482,7767,5127,38357,23606,11,323,582,646,11625,432,1667,279,40565,21963,50078,40325,382,5338,11,582,3796,279,21963,50078,40325,311,1477,279,12196,4185,49109,320,91289,8,315,220,16,21,15,323,220,16,18,24,24,1447,16,13,17767,220,16,18,24,24,284,220,23,1124,50853,220,16,21,15,488,220,16,20,24,1124,340,17,13,17767,220,16,21,15,284,220,16,1124,50853,220,16,20,24,488,220,16,1124,340,18,13,17767,220,16,20,24,284,220,16,20,24,1124,50853,220,16,488,220,15,1124,692],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nNotice that  \\[35\\cdot40=1400.\\]Find some integer $n$ with $0\\leq n<1399$ such that $n$ is the multiplicative inverse to 160 modulo 1399.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"573fd87b4d92083500ee5ec937bcacba564bcbc090fcb4d0ee3b1856b96667ef"},"seconds":16.84940814971924,"seed":507544976179589968,"stop":"eos","text":"The gcd is 1, which confirms that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and thus an inverse exists. Now we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399.\n\nFrom the Euclidean Algorithm steps:\n\n1. \\( 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\)\n2. \\( 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( 159 \\) from the second equation into the first equation:\n\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis equation shows that \\( 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\). Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is \\( 9 \\).\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{9}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nThe gcd is 1, which confirms that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and thus an inverse exists. Now we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399.\n\nFrom the Euclidean Algorithm steps:\n\n1. \\( 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\)\n2. \\( 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\)\n\nSubstitute \\( 159 \\) from the second equation into the first equation:\n\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis equation shows that \\( 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\). Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is \\( 9 \\).\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]","id":"number_theory:231"}
